netkit is a BPF-programmable virtual network device introduced in Linux
6.7 by the Cilium team. It is designed as a next-generation replacement
for veth in cloud-native / container networking scenarios, allowing BPF
programs to be attached directly to the device transmission routine and
operating in either L2 or L3 mode.

Enabling CONFIG_NETKIT is required by:
- tools/testing/selftests/net/nk_qlease.py and the in-tree netkit
  selftests (tools/testing/selftests/net/config explicitly sets
  CONFIG_NETKIT=y).
- Cilium >= 1.14 netkit device mode, see:
  https://docs.cilium.io/en/stable/operations/system_requirements/
  ("The netkit device mode requires CONFIG_NETKIT=y").

Note CONFIG_NETKIT is a bool (not tristate) and depends on
CONFIG_BPF_SYSCALL, so it can only be set to y or n. Move it from
L2-OPTIONAL to L1-RECOMMEND with value y on all architectures.
